Q: How do I get my hyperlink leading to another page to play an audio sound when I click it? A: <p>The solution to the problem you raise involves “tricking” the visitor. Let's see how:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>document.getElementById( "false_hiperlink" ).addEventListener( "click", function() {   
   code_sound();
   code_xx();       
});

function code_sound() {
   console.log( "pipi" );
}

function code_xx() {
   console.log( "go to other" );
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* This is the fundamental line, change the color of the text 
   so that it looks like a hyperlink. */
#false_hiperlink {
   color : blue;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;p id="false_hiperlink"&gt; go to -&gt; &lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How do you use a c string (char array) in GCC with inline ARM assembly A: <p>The system register values are encoded into the instruction and this happens at compile time.<br />
Here the question boils down to &quot;<em><strong>Can we insert new instructions are run time ?</strong></em>&quot;. You could write a very much non-portable solution which involves creating instructions during runtime and updating an executable region of ram and running it. see my below example (I have not tested this, but this should convey my idea)</p>
<p>We leverage the ARM ABI, which says the return value will be on X0, so reading the value into X0 and returning will fulfill your wish. Also we cannot directly encode the value as string, but use the SRE representation of the registers (registers can be represented a series of numbers)</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/WxeiCTDw.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">See this image showing SRE values for CNTP_CTL_EL0</a></p>
<p><strong>Keep in mind, having code like this production is a security nightmare waiting to happen.</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

#define INST_RET 0xD65F03C0
#define INST_MRS 0xD53

#define FORMAT_SRE(op0, op1, CRn, CRm, op2) ((uint32_t)((0x3 &amp; op0) &lt;&lt; 18)) | \
                                            ((uint32_t)((0x7 &amp; op1) &lt;&lt; 15)) | \
                                            ((uint32_t)((0xf &amp; CRn) &lt;&lt; 11)) | \
                                            ((uint32_t)((0xf &amp; CRm) &lt;&lt; 7 )) | \
                                            ((uint32_t)((0x7 &amp; op2) &lt;&lt; 4 ))

#define FORMAT_INS(op0, op1, CRn, CRm, op2, Rt) ((uint32_t)INST_MRS &lt;&lt; 20) | \
                                                ((uint32_t)FORMAT_SRE(op0, op1, CRn, CRm, op2)) | \
                                                Rt


typedef uint32_t (*asm_msr_t)();

int main()
{
    printf(&quot;Test %X\n&quot;, FORMAT_INS(3,5,14,2,1,0));
    /*
     * Get some memory in ram which has executable permision
     * Let asume the memory 0x4000000 is atleast 16 bytes long and has read, write and executable permision
     * If in embedded env. may be you can hardcode some memory in linker, 
     * this only needs to be 8 to 16 bytes long and need to be 4 byte aligned (for Arm64).
     *
     * In linux you could do something like
     * void *buf = mmap(NULL, 4096, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE | PROT_EXEC, MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
     */

    asm_msr_t code = (asm_msr_t)0x4000000;
    uint32_t *instruction = (uint32_t*)0x4000000;

    instruction[0] = (uint32_t)FORMAT_INS(3,5,14,2,1,0);
    instruction[1] = (uint32_t)INST_RET;
    __builtin___clear_cache((char*)instruction, (char*)(instruction + 2));

    uint32_t val = code();

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
